      "context": {
        "kind": "context",
        "subject_id": "CTX232-ERRORS",
        "content_hash": "a5fbee3f59896068813ac6982abd0a4be9f4d30870d4b2bb4bf81084720e4bcc"
      },
      "natural_statement": "Under the finite-error hypotheses, weighted mixing mass and dispersion defect remain summable, exceptional physical lines have finite mass, and endcaps have the bound 6*B*h/b plus the rotated error variation. Deleting the central line makes the summed error corridors decrease to the empty set, even for atomic errors. Tangential-band scaling eliminates both finite errors and punctured alias neighborhoods.",
      "formal_statement": "",
      "external_assumptions": [],
      "retired_assumptions": [],
      "content_hash": "ecc9955736bc955939316ace00fd7ec0e5ccca55b69fa16404e837c3dd762044"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "S232-LINE-CORRECTION",
      "version": 1,
      "context": {
        "kind": "context",
        "subject_id": "CTX232-ERRORS",
        "content_hash": "a5fbee3f59896068813ac6982abd0a4be9f4d30870d4b2bb4bf81084720e4bcc"
      },
      "natural_statement": "For finite signed chart errors the full raw scalar limit L exists, but the coefficient is c=-2*(L+B_0)/pi, where B_0=integral_{k=0}Q dC is finite. B_0=0 for the original exact-mixture hypotheses. The six-point atomic orbit proves that omitting B_0 in the larger class gives an incorrect coefficient.",
      "formal_statement": "",
      "external_assumptions": [],
      "retired_assumptions": [],
      "content_hash": "d3217eeee10a05630bca521e89e46ca5a1c5c70c8fc7afcc9f464f9df874e0ce"
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "S232-ATOMIC-WITNESS",
      "version": 1,
      "context": {
        "kind": "context",
        "subject_id": "CTX232-ERRORS",
        "content_hash": "a5fbee3f59896068813ac6982abd0a4be9f4d30870d4b2bb4bf81084720e4bcc"
      },
      "natural_statement": "The six unit atoms at R_{pi/3}^j(0,1/8), j=0,...,5, are D6 invariant, have zero spectral tangent, raw cutoff limit -2, and exceptional-line counterterm +2 when Q=1 on their radius. They refute only the uncorrected extension to finite spectral errors, not an original manuscript statement.",
      "formal_statement": "",
      "external_assumptions": [],
      "retired_assumptions": [],
      "content_hash": "9830bf1b966f6211ff8913ba19f19c3fae5450d41979ee38dd0230731b70d52a"
    }
  ],
